  "guidePatchSlotKeyboard": "Keyboard",
  "guidePatchSlotKeyboardDesc": "Plays a piano or any sound from a soundfont (.sf2). Set it to None (MIDI only) to use it only to send notes to other modules. A key touched near its front edge plays louder than near the back; Preferences > Key velocity can fix one strength instead.",
  "guidePatchSlotVocoder": "Vocoder",
  "guidePatchSlotVocoderDesc": "Your voice through the microphone becomes a synth that plays the notes you hold.",
  "guidePatchSlotJam": "Jam Mode",
  "guidePatchSlotJamDesc": "Keeps one keyboard in the scale of the chords another keyboard plays.",
  "guidePatchSlotVst3": "VST3 Plugin",
  "guidePatchSlotVst3Desc": "Loads a VST3 instrument or effect installed on your computer.",
  "guidePatchSlotVst3DesktopOnly": "VST3 plugins work on Linux, macOS and Windows only, not on Android or in the web demo.",
  "guidePatchTitle": "The cable view",
  "guidePatchIntro": "Tap the cable icon at the top of the rack to see the back of every module. Tap the back arrow to return. Modules cannot be moved in this view.",
  "guidePatchDrawTitle": "Drawing a cable",
  "guidePatchDrawBody": "Long-press an output socket and drag. Sockets that accept the cable light up. Release on one to connect, anywhere else to cancel. An output only connects to an input of the same colour.",
  "guidePatchDisconnectTitle": "Removing a cable",
  "guidePatchDisconnectBody": "Tap the small ✕ in the middle of a cable, then Disconnect. Removing a module removes its cables too.",
  "guidePatchDataTitle": "Scale and tuning cables",
  "guidePatchDataBody": "Purple cables carry harmony. CHORD OUT into CHORD IN tells Jam Mode or Xen which keyboard's chords to follow. SCALE OUT into SCALE IN adds a keyboard that follows the scale. The cables and the menus in the panels always agree.\nTeal TUNE OUT into TUNE IN carries a Xen scale's tuning to an instrument. It is separate from the scale cable, so you can keep a keyboard in a scale without retuning it, or the other way round.",
  "guidePatchSharedEffectTitle": "One effect, one signal",
  "guidePatchSharedEffectBody": "An effect remembers the sound passing through it (a reverb tail, a delay line), so it can only process one signal. Two keyboards cabled straight into the same reverb is fine: they are mixed first. One keyboard straight into a reverb and another through a harmonizer into the same reverb is two different signals, and GrooveForge refuses the cable.",
  "guidePatchSharedEffectWorkaround": "If a cable is refused for this reason, add a second copy of the effect and cable the other signal into it.",
  "guidePatchTip": "Cables are saved with the project.",
  "guideXenTitle": "Xen: scales and tunings",
  "guideXenBody": "Xen keeps every note you play inside a scale. Hold a key and tap a scale: the key you hold becomes the root note, until you do it again. Picking a scale also switches Xen on.\nSNAP moves notes that are outside the scale to the nearest one that fits; the piano greys them out. TUNE plays the scale's own tuning, for scales whose notes fall between the piano keys: those keys get a teal band and their offset in cents. SNAP and TUNE switch on and off separately.\nThe Family menu has Western modes, Arabic and Turkish maqamat, Indian ragas, Chinese, Japanese and Korean scales, gamelan tunings, historical temperaments and more. My Scales holds the scales you build or import from Scala (.scl) files.",
  "guideJamModeTitle": "Jam Mode",
  "guideJamModeBody": "Jam Mode is for two keyboards: one plays chords, the other improvises. In its panel, MASTER is the keyboard whose chords set the scale, and TARGETS are the keyboards that follow. Every note a target plays moves into that scale. You can also draw the link in the cable view: CHORD OUT into CHORD IN, SCALE OUT into SCALE IN.",
  "guideVocoderTitle": "Vocoder",
  "guideVocoderBody": "Tap + and pick Vocoder. Sing or talk into the microphone while you hold notes: your voice shapes a synth that plays them. Set the microphone level with the gain knobs. Try the carrier waves (Saw, Square, Choral), or Harmony, which drops the synth and moves your own voice onto the keys you hold.",
  "guideThereminTitle": "Theremin and Stylophone",
  "guideThereminBody": "The Theremin has two modes. PAD is a touch pad: slide up and down for pitch, left and right for volume. CAM (Android, iOS and macOS) uses the front camera to sense how far your hand is: closer plays higher, far enough away is silent, and volume stays full. BASE sets the lowest note, RANGE the span in octaves, VIB the vibrato and HEIGHT the size of the pad.\nThe Stylophone is a 25-key strip you play by touching or sliding along it. It plays one note at a time, like the original, and glides between keys without a click. SQR, SAW, SIN and TRI pick the waveform.",
  "guideDrumGeneratorTitle": "Drum Generator",
  "guideDrumGeneratorBody": "Plays a drum pattern at the transport tempo, from bossa nova to metal. Swing pushes the off-beats late. Human feel goes from a strict grid to a drummer who breathes. Count-in plays a few hits before the pattern starts, and Fill every adds a fill every few bars.\nSong form lays out a whole song as blocks (intro, verse, chorus, bridge, break, ending). Tap + to add one, tap a block to edit it, long-press to move it. Without an ending block the form loops; with one, the drums end the song and stop the transport.\nPatterns are .gfdrum files, so you can add your own.",
  "guideLoopersTitle": "Loopers",
  "guideLoopersBody": "The MIDI Looper records the notes you play and loops them. The Audio Looper records sound, and can also overdub and reverse.\nBoth wait for the next bar to start recording and round the loop to whole bars, so loops stay in time with each other.\nThe Audio Looper records what is cabled into its AUDIO IN: cable an instrument or a Live Input into it first.",
  "guideMidiFxTitle": "MIDI FX",
  "guideMidiFxBody": "MIDI FX change the notes on their way from a keyboard to its sound. Harmonizer adds up to four voices to what you play; with Scale Lock on, they stay in the current scale. Chord Expand turns one note into a chord. Arpeggiator plays held notes one after another, in time. Transposer shifts everything by up to two octaves. Velocity Curve changes how loud soft and hard notes come out. Gate lets through only notes within a velocity or pitch range. Microtone merges held notes into one microtonal pitch.",
  "guideMidiTitle": "Connect a controller",
  "guideMidiBody": "GrooveForge works with USB MIDI keyboards and pads (through an OTG adapter on a phone) and with Bluetooth MIDI devices.",
  "guideMidiBestPracticeTitle": "One keyboard or two?",
  "guideMidiBestPracticeBody": "One keyboard is enough: a new project pairs a GrooveForge Keyboard with a Xen module. Xen starts off and switches on when you pick a scale: hold a note and tap a scale button.\nA second keyboard, or a controller split in two, is useful for Jam Mode: one hand plays the chords that set the scale, the other improvises over them.",
  "guideSoundfontsTitle": "Sounds (soundfonts)",
  "guideSoundfontsBody": "The keyboard plays its sounds from soundfont files (.sf2). To add one, open Preferences, then under Soundfonts tap Load Soundfont (.sf2). It then appears in the sound menu of every keyboard. A good soundfont can take several hundred MB.",
  "guideTipsTitle": "Improvising",
  "guideTipsBody": "New to improvising? A few ideas:\n• In a scale, every key fits the music. There are no wrong notes, only surprising ones.\n• Start or end your phrases on the root note: it sounds like coming home.\n• Rhythm first. A simple idea played in time beats a fast run.\n• Leave space. Short phrases with rests between them sound more musical than a stream of notes.",
